\paragraph{MAX-SAT instance.} Each unique constraint $c_m$ serves as a soft clause with weight $w_m$. The objective is to find the largest-weight satisfiable subset:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:maxsat}
\mathcal{C}^* = \arg\max_{\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{C}} \sum_{c \in \mathcal{S}} w_c \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathcal{S} \text{ is satisfiable.}
\end{equation}
Z3's optimization engine solves this with a 10-second per-problem timeout.

\paragraph{Answer scoring.} Let $\mathcal{C}^{\setminus} = \mathcal{C} \setminus \mathcal{C}^*$ denote the excluded constraint set. Each candidate answer $a \in \mathcal{A}(q)$ is scored by:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:score}
\text{score}(a) = \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^*} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a) \;-\; \alpha \sum_{c \in \mathcal{C}^{\setminus}} w_c \cdot \sigma(c, a),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(c, a) \in \{0, 1\}$ is a source-trace indicator: $\sigma(c, a) = 1$ if $c$ was extracted from any trace that produced answer $a$, and 0 otherwise. The penalty weight $\alpha = 0.5$ in all experiments. The first term rewards answers whose source constraints are satisfied; the second penalizes answers whose source constraints were excluded by MAX-SAT. Discriminative signal arises when constraints from different traces conflict: Z3 excludes low-weight constraints from $\mathcal{C}^*$, and the scorer attributes these exclusions to the corresponding source answers via $\sigma$ (see Algorithm~2 in Appendix~\ref{app:z3-pseudocode}). To ensure each candidate answer participates in scoring, the pipeline injects an \emph{answer assertion} constraint $\texttt{answer\_a} \mathrel{{=}{=}} \texttt{True}$ for each trace, with weight 1. This guarantees that every candidate receives a nonzero base score proportional to its vote count; SICA is therefore a strict generalization of SC. The selected answer is $a^* = \arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \text{score}(a)$.

\paragraph{Degeneration under non-discriminative constraints.}
In practice, self-extracted constraints are tightly coupled to their source answer: $c_m$ derived from trace $t_i$ typically entails $a_i$ ($\sigma(c_m, a_i) \approx 1$). When constraints lack discriminative power across candidates, MAX-SAT excludes almost no constraints ($|\mathcal{C}^{\setminus}| \approx 0$), so the penalty term vanishes regardless of $\alpha$, and the score function degenerates:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:degenerate}
\text{score}(a) \approx \sum_{\substack{c \in \mathcal{C}^* \\ \text{source}(c) \to a}} w_c,
\end{equation}
which reduces to a weighted count of traces supporting $a$, equivalent to majority voting when weights are uniform. Even direct per-answer Z3 evaluation of self-extracted constraints would yield the same outcome, since only 11.5\% of constraints evaluate differently across candidates (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). The ablation in \S\ref{sec:ablation} confirms this empirically: MAX-SAT, count-only, and random-weight baselines produce nearly identical accuracy.

\subsection{The $C_0$ Diagnostic}
\label{sec:c0}

We define the multi-candidate rate $C_0$ as the fraction of questions for which $K$ sampled traces produce more than one distinct answer:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:c0}
C_0 = \frac{|\{q \in \mathcal{Q} : |\mathcal{A}(q)| > 1\}|}{|\mathcal{Q}|}.
\end{equation}

$C_0$ is a zero-cost diagnostic computable before any constraint extraction. When $C_0$ falls below 5\%, nearly all questions are answered unanimously, and SICA produces the same output as SC by construction. High $C_0$ does not guarantee improvement, as it merely ensures disagreement exists without ensuring constraints can resolve it. $C_0$ serves as a necessary-condition screening criterion for practitioners considering symbolic aggregation.

\subsection{Scope and Limitations}
\label{sec:scope}

SICA as evaluated here uses \emph{same-model self-extraction}: the same LLM generates traces and extracts constraints. This isolates whether an LLM's own reasoning can be usefully formalized for aggregation. Our evaluation does not cover trained extractors, external provers, or cross-model setups. Published methods achieving positive results through different mechanisms \citep{xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, luo2025logicagent} are complementary to our findings. An oracle variant (\S\ref{sec:oracle}) disentangles extraction quality from aggregation effectiveness.

